# Wireframes

## What is a wireframe?
#### A wireframe is a low-fidelity, structural layout showing content and functionality, without visual design.

<div style="text-align: center; width: 80%; margin: 0 auto;">
  <p style="
    font-size: 18px; 
    background-color: #F5F7F8; 
    padding: 10px; 
    border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; 
    border-right: 5px solid #8148B2;
    display: inline-block;
  ">
    Just like an architect sketches a blueprint before building, designers use wireframes to plan a website’s structure before coding.
  </p>
</div>


#### Wireframe sketches are intentionally simple so it’s easy to change ideas and adjust layouts without worrying about colors, images, or detailed design.

<img src="https://images.code.org/1f42cada9ca13c98b7dd4d67ba13d231-image-1761150656423.png" alt="Sample sketched wireframe" width="850px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
<p style="font-size: 8pt; color: #555; margin-top: 8px;">
Image source: <a href="https://www.springboard.com/blog/design/how-to-create-a-wireframe/" target="_blank" style="color: #0077cc; text-decoration: none;">Springboard – How to Create a Wireframe</a>
</p>

#### At this stage, the focus is on what goes where—like where a button, heading, or image might appear—rather than how it looks. Keeping things low-fidelity encourages feedback and quick revisions before time is spent on visuals or code.

## Sketch Lab Sample
#### Check out this example made in **Sketch Lab**, our built-in visual design tool. Like Canva or Figma, it allows students to create their designs right on the platform—no extra tools needed!

<img src="https://images.code.org/366ecab8f8cd0f0c8138c45fc86e687e-image-1761152722160.png" alt="Sample sketched wireframe" width="850px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15)">


## Let’s practice
#### Now it's your turn to draw a wireframe in Sketch Lab.


